{"A": {"correctness":2,"depth":6,"instruction_following":7,"practicality":2,"overall":4.0,"rationale":"A contains a fatal early-return bug in the worker loop that terminates workers upon their first successful job, meaning it will never process more jobs than the initial concurrency limit. Additionally, the abortableSleep helper is left unfinished and contains comments admitting it is not fully implemented."},"B": {"correctness":4,"depth":5,"instruction_following":6,"practicality":4,"overall":4.7,"rationale":"B will crash on cancellation because aborting during semaphore acquisition or retry backoff throws an unhandled rejection, causing Promise.all to reject and bypass the result aggregation entirely. It also incorrectly uses the current epoch timestamp instead of elapsed duration for onItemSettled."},"C": {"correctness":7,"depth":7,"instruction_following":7,"practicality":8,"overall":7.2,"rationale":"C uses a clean worker pool structure and runs successfully, but it leaves a messy, unused sleepMs function in the source code and leaks timers in TokenBucket.sleep on abort due to clearing the wrong timeout ID. The success result array also fails to include the item or original index, making it impossible to correlate successful outputs to their inputs."},"comparison":"B and C do not give up quality compared to A; rather, they are significantly more functional because A contains a fatal loop-termination bug that prevents it from processing more than the initial concurrency batch. While B offers a better result schema with input-output mapping, it crashes on cancellation, whereas C runs reliably and uses a clean worker pool but suffers from a timer leak and lacks output correlation."}
